<exercise xml:id="exercise_4_4_10">
<statement>
<p>
Below you see graphs of the functions <m>\displaystyle y=x^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>,   <m>\displaystyle y=2x^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>, and <m>\displaystyle y=-x^{\frac{1}{2}}</m>. Decide which is which.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ex_4_4_11">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ex_4_4_11_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-0.5,xmax=3,
ymin=-3,ymax=3,
legend pos = south east,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel={}, ylabel={},
]
\addplot[domain=0:3] {2*sqrt(x)};
\addlegendentry{A}
\addplot[domain=0:3,red] {sqrt(x)};
\addlegendentry{B}
\addplot[domain=0:3,orange] {-sqrt(x)};
\addlegendentry{C}
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Graph A is <m>y=2 x^{1/2}</m>, graph B is <m>y=x^{1/2}</m>, and graph C is <m>y=-x^{1/2}</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="exercise_4_4_11">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for a power function <m>f(x)=kx^p</m> given numerically by:
</p>


<table>
<title></title>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>3\sqrt{2}</m></cell>
<cell><m>3\sqrt{3}</m></cell>
<cell><m>3\sqrt{5}</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>f(x)=3x^\frac{1}{2}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="exercise_4_4_12">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for a power function <m>g(x)=kx^p</m> given numerically by: 
</p>

<table>
<title></title>
<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
<cell>undefined</cell>
<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell><m>\frac{2}{3}</m></cell>
<cell><m>\frac{2}{5}</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>


</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<m>g(x)=2x^{-1}</m>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="exercise_4_4_13">
<introduction>
<p>
Consider a pendulum depicted below
<fn>Modified from a public domain image at <url href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pendulum">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pendulum</url>, accessed: 7/8/20</fn>.
</p>

<image source="images/exercise-4.4-pendulum.png" width="40%">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
</image>


<p>
When the pendulum is displaced sideways from its resting position<mdash/>called the equilibrium position<mdash/>the force due to gravity will cause the pendulum to oscillate back and forth about the equilibrium position. The time, <m>T</m>, needed to execute one full cycle<mdash/>a left swing and the right swing<mdash/>is called the pendulum's period. The period <m>T</m> depends on the length <m>L</m> of the pendulum
<fn><url href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pendulum">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pendulum</url>, accessed: 7/8/20</fn>
and the local acceleration due to gravity <m>g</m>:
<md>
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}.
</md>
</p>
</introduction>

<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
        With <m>g</m> fixed, is <m>T</m> a power function of <m>L</m>? In other words, is <m>T=kL^p</m>? If yes, find the coefficient and the exponent.
        </p>
    </statement>
<solution>
    <p>yes; <m>T=\dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt{g}}L^\frac{1}{2}</m> where the coefficient is <m>\dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt{g}}</m> and the exponent is <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
        On the surface of the Earth, the acceleration due to gravity, <m>g</m>, is equal to <m>9.81\text{ m}/\text{sec}^2</m> where m stands for meters. Calculate the period of a pendulum of length <m>0.5 \text{ m}</m> that happily oscillates in Kingston, RI.
        </p>
    </statement>
<solution>
    <p>
        approximately <m>1.42</m> seconds.
    </p>
</solution>
</task>

<task>
    <statement>
        <p>
        An astronaut is standing on the surface of a faraway asteroid wondering about the acceleration due to gravity on the asteroid. The astronaut has a watch and a pendulum whose length is <m>0.3 \text{ m}</m>. The astronaut measures the period of the pendulum which turns out to be <m>5</m> seconds. What is the acceleration due to gravity on the asteroid?
        </p>
    </statement>
<solution>
    <p>
        approximately <m>0.47 \text{ m}/\text{sec}^2</m>.
    </p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>


</exercises>